**Component 2a — Credit Check object (Equifax/CRI persistence) — tonight's light build:**

> **What it represents:** the *stored result of one Equifax call* (the receipt/snapshot), NOT the live API call. The Apex callout action is built later; this is just the filing cabinet.
> **Naming principle:** plain-English labels (analysts + panel read them cold); where a word is a compliance term of art (enquiry, retention), keep it in help text.

- Object **`Credit_Check__c`** (label "Credit Check"), **master-detail → `Loan_Application__c`** (owned by the assessment; inherits parent sharing).
  - **🎁 Master-detail + cascade vs audit (deck reconciliation):** two lifecycle phases — *during retention* the app is NOT deleted (governance/policy keeps it → audit trail intact); *at retention end* the app is deleted → **cascade removes CRI + all children together** = clean Part IIIA disposal. Cascade isn't accidental data loss; it's the scheduled-destruction mechanism. Precision: **master-detail = the *how*** (no orphans when deletion happens); **retention policy = the *when*** (deletion only on schedule). Together = "retain then destroy."
- Fields (minimise — decision-relevant only) + **classification** (CRI = one tier **stricter** than Component 1's Restricted → **MissionCritical**, because Part IIIA is a distinct legal regime, not just more sensitive data — the schema self-documents that):

| Field | Type | Classification |
|---|---|---|
| `Credit_Score__c` | Number | 🔴🔴 MissionCritical (core CRI) |
| `Credit_Check_Reference__c` | Text | 🔴🔴 MissionCritical (help text: "bureau enquiry reference") |
| `Full_Credit_Report__c` | Long Text (optional/controlled) | 🔴🔴 MissionCritical (full payload = most exposure) |
| `Credit_Check_Date__c` | DateTime | Operational (audit metadata) |
| `Delete_After_Date__c` | Date | Operational (help text: "Part IIIA retention expiry") |
| `Is_Latest__c` | Checkbox | Operational |

  - *Panel line:* "CRI fields are MissionCritical — one tier above ordinary financial PII — because Part IIIA is a distinct legal regime, not just more sensitive data. The classification makes that visible in the schema so credit data can't be mistaken for a normal financial field."
- **Own permission set `Loan Application CRI`** — separate from Base/Sensitive because Part IIIA credit reporting info has its own access rules; even an analyst's CRI access is a distinct, auditable grant.
  - **DECISION (locked): include CRI in the `Credit_Analyst` PSG** — at PrimeCapital, consumer-lending analysts need CRI to do the job, so bundle it for one-assignment convenience. Kept as its own *permission set* (component) so the grant is still a discrete, auditable artifact even inside the group.
  - **Future-separation trigger (line for deck + build):** *if* a role emerges that does NOT need CRI — **business/commercial-only analysts** (Part IIIA = consumer credit; different regime), **trainee/intake/QA** roles, or **consent-gap** cases — split `Loan Application CRI` into its own PSG (`Credit_Analyst_CRI`) so CRI becomes a separate grant. The permission-set boundary already exists, so this is a low-cost change.
  - **Panel framing:** "Most consumer-lending analysts need CRI, so I bundled it — but kept it a distinct permission set. If a non-CRI role appears (business-only, trainee, consent-gap), I separate it into its own group so Part IIIA access stays provable. The seam is already there."

**🎁 INSERT-ONLY decision (deck):** each Equifax pull = a **new immutable record**, never upsert/overwrite.
- *Why:* each pull is a distinct **auditable event** — you must reconstruct what the decision was based on. Overwriting destroys that (June pull erases the March one → can't answer "what did you see when you approved loan #123?").
- *Find "current"* by latest Report Retrieved Date (or `Is_Latest__c`), never by overwriting.

**🎁 "Why a record every time? Credit doesn't change often" (anticipated Q — defense):**
- Reframe: *not storing the score repeatedly — recording each **enquiry** as an event.* Even with an identical score, two pulls differ: different timestamp, consent, purpose, Equifax enquiry reference; each is a **hard enquiry** that legally occurred.
- Cost/enquiry-harm is managed by **when you call** (pull once per assessment, reuse — avoid redundant hard enquiries), NOT by how you store. Two axes: *when to call* (efficiency) vs *how to store* (auditability).
- *Line:* "The score may not change, but each enquiry is a separate legal event with its own consent, timestamp and bureau reference. Overwriting would destroy the Part IIIA audit trail. I control cost by not re-pulling within an assessment — but never collapse two real enquiries into one record."

**🎁 FIELD HISTORY = wrong reflex here (deck):** records are insert-only → nothing changes → field history has nothing to track. The Part IIIA concern is **access** (who *viewed* CRI) → handled by Event Monitoring / access log, and **who pulled** → the callout's custom log. Use field history ONLY on retention/destruction fields (to evidence disposal on schedule).
- ⏳ Component 2 task: **access log** (who pulls AND who views CRI) — separate from this object.

**Component 2a-log — CRI Access Log object (tonight: schema only):**
- Object **`CRI_Access_Log__c`** (label "CRI Access Log"). Fields: `Credit_Check__c` (Lookup → Credit Check), `Loan_Application__c` (Lookup), `Access_Type__c` (Picklist: Pull/View), `Accessed_By__c` (Lookup → User), `Access_Timestamp__c` (DateTime).
- **Insert-only** (same platform-enforced pattern as Credit Check: object Edit=false, Delete=false; field Edit=true for insert). FLS on `Loan Application CRI` permission set.
- **🎁 Lookup, NOT master-detail (deliberate contrast):** Credit Check = master-detail (cascades at retention end — it's part of the assessment). Access Log = **Lookup** → must **survive** even if the app/credit-check is deleted. An audit trail that vanishes with the thing it audits is worthless. *Panel line:* "The audit trail is decoupled from what it audits at BOTH levels — transaction (Platform Events) and data model (lookup, not master-detail) — so the operation it records can't destroy it."
- **🎁 Platform Event for rollback-survival (decision — build in 2b):** a synchronous log DML dies if the main transaction rolls back. Write the log via a **Platform Event with `Publish Immediately`** — immediate-publish events commit **independently of the transaction**, so the log survives a rollback. (`Publish After Commit` and `@future`/Queueable do NOT survive a pre-commit rollback.) Honest caveat: immediate-publish = *decoupled + retried*, not literally unloseable (the subscriber's insert is its own transaction). Build the event + subscriber in **Component 2b** alongside the Equifax callout (the publisher) — wiring the event before its publisher exists is backwards. Tonight = object only.

**🎁 STRUCTURAL IMMUTABILITY (deck gem — from the build):** insert-only is enforced by the **permission model, not convention/Apex.** Two levers: **object-level Edit = false** → no UPDATE DML can ever happen (platform rejects it); **field-level Edit = true** → values can still be set at INSERT. Combined = insert allowed, update impossible, no code. Delete also withheld (records leave only via parent cascade at retention end). *Line:* "Immutability is structural, not conventional — the permission set grants no object Edit, so the platform physically refuses any update to a credit-check record. Proof is live: I try to update one and Salesforce rejects it." Upgrades the story from "we don't update" to "we can't."
- **⚠️ CAVEAT (must state — don't overclaim):** `Modify All Data` / `Modify All` **bypasses** object-level Edit (admin update succeeded in test). So immutability holds for the **operational (analyst) population**, with the super-user bypass being a **deliberately-restricted, audited exception**. *Line:* "Insert-only holds for normal users; it's overridden by Modify All Data, which is exactly why that permission is tightly held and audited — you don't design against your own break-glass admin, you audit them." (Stronger + honest vs "nobody can update it.")
- **Detail (User-lookup lifecycle):** a lookup to User can only be `SetNull` on delete (users are deactivated, never deleted), which forces the field optional. Bonus: the access log **survives** its subject — if the accessing user is deactivated, the log keeps a null user ref rather than being deleted. Audit outlives its subjects.
- **⏳ TASK (light, ~15 min — promotes "open thread" to action): non-admin insert-only PROOF.** Create a test user with the `Credit_Analyst` PSG (NO Modify All Data) → as that user, insert a Credit Check / Access Log (succeeds) → attempt update (**platform rejects**) → screenshot/record. This is the *demonstrable* version (like the 6→17 reveal) and validates BOTH CRI objects at once. Currently only *configured*, not *proven*.

