## Panel Talking Points — Trade-offs to Raise (running log)

> Accumulates across phases. These are the "thought process + trade-off" moments to surface *live* in the presentation — where showing **how** I chose matters more than the choice itself. Each phase appends its own.

**Action #1 — Retrieve FSC profile (Named Query API)**
1. **Release maturity / version dependency.** Named Query API only reached GA recently (Spring/Summer '26, API v66–67). I'd confirm the org is on v66+ before committing; if not, fall back to Flow (Get Records) or Apex invocable. *Trade-off:* the newest option is simplest + best-performing, but carries a version dependency the older paths don't. *(A panellist may test whether I know it's recent.)*
2. **Parameter boundary.** Inputs are supported in **WHERE and LIMIT only**. Fine for a lookup by ID/filter — but I'd flag the limit so no one designs a query needing parameterised OFFSET or sub-queries.
3. **"Full FSC profile" spans multiple objects.** An FSC profile isn't one record (Account/Person Account + financial accounts + related objects). *Design choice:* one relationship SOQL query vs. several actions. *Trade-off:* single query = fewer round-trips but more complex/brittle SOQL; multiple = simpler each, more orchestration.
4. **Security is the designer's responsibility — and a selling point.** Named Query runs with the invoking user's **read access** (Summer '26 also defaults Apex to user mode). *Design point:* filtered queries + correct permission sets, never an unbounded fetch. *Doubles as* a least-privilege / Trust-Layer story for the CRO.

*Mechanism options considered (slide-ready):*

| Option | Status | Trade-off |
|---|---|---|
| **Named Query API** | GA (v66+) | Simplest + fastest + permission-aware — **primary** |
| Flow + Get Records | GA | Admin-friendly fallback; more moving parts |
| Apex invocable + SOQL | GA | Most flexible; pro-code overhead |

**Grounding — source-to-pattern split (actions 2–4)**
5. **Four sources, three patterns — "not everything is RAG."** FSC profile = fetch (SOQL), Equifax = fetch (API), financial statements = extract, policy PDFs = RAG. *Lead line:* I matched the retrieval pattern to the task, not one-size-fits-all.
6. **Extract vs RAG defence (likely probe).** *Q a panellist asks:* "statements are in Data Cloud as unstructured data like the policy PDFs — why extract, not RAG?" *Answer:* RAG = "search prose when I don't know where the answer is"; extract = "pull THE value when I know exactly what I need." A credit memo needs precise figures, not plausible chunks. Split is driven by the *task*, not where the doc lives.
7. **Benchmark reports = a lurking 5th source.** Environment lists industry benchmark reports in Data Cloud, not in the numbered Ask. I'd RAG these for peer comparison — noticing it shows I read the whole brief.
8. **Chunking the policy PDFs (all doc-verified, official Salesforce Help).**
   - **Section-aware chunking** — the *default* strategy for PDFs; uses headings, keeps a section together, never splits mid-sentence. Perfect for heading-structured policy docs.
   - **Max token = 512 by default.** For English/Latin, ~1 word ≈ 1 token, so default is fine. For **non-Latin** content, set *below* 512 (Data 360 counts punctuation marks for non-Latin, which can overshoot real tokens and drop text from the embedding).
   - **Overlap tokens** — only kicks in when one section is big enough to split into multiple chunks; adds end-of-chunk tokens to the next chunk for smoother retrieval.
   - **Tuning lever + trade-off:** max token is also bounded by the embedding model (SFR-v2-small / Salesforce Embedding V2 Small supports up to 1,200). If policy *sections run longer than 512 tokens*, raise max token (up to the model limit) to keep a full clause intact — trading some retrieval precision for more context per chunk.
9. **How to CHOOSE chunking + parsing (strong reframe).** Driven by *format + parser + what survives parsing + tables* — not how granular a doc looks.
   - **Parsing happens BEFORE chunking.** Data 360 has 3 parsers: Default Parser, **Docling**, LLM-based visual pre-processing (official — Salesforce Trailhead). For born-digital policy PDFs with tables → **Docling** (reconstructs tables to Markdown/JSON) or **LLM-based visual pre-processing** (prepares tables/images for chunking, preserving table relationships). ← this is the real lever for the table problem, not chunking strategy.
   - **Semantic-based passage extraction is HTML-tag-based** (`<h1–h6>`, `<b>`, `<p>`…). A raw PDF has no tags — but Docling *can emit HTML/Markdown*, so semantic-based on a *parsed* PDF is plausible though NOT confirmed in the chunking docs. **Documented default for PDF = section-aware.** Panel-safe line: "section-aware is the documented PDF default; I'd validate semantic-based on parsed PDFs rather than assume."
   - **Tables carry critical logic** (approval authority by $ threshold, ratio limits). Handled at the PARSE step (Docling / LLM-visual), not by prose chunking. Code Extension = fallback for truly custom needs.
   - **Strategy + parser set per search index, not per document** — pick one for a homogeneous corpus; split indexes only if formats differ.
   - Demonstrated via a 2-PDF comparison (RAA = clean headings+prose; CHRC = headings+bold+**tables** with $ thresholds).
   - **Meta talking point:** resolved a conflict between two AI answers (mine vs Agentforce) by going to official docs → *"here's what's documented, here's what I'd test"* beats any confident assertion. Models the doc-grounding discipline live.
10. **Pre-filtering the 120 policy PDFs (doc-verified — Salesforce Trailhead).**
    - Custom retriever supports **Filter Documents to Return** — up to 10 conditions, AND/OR, each = a *field from the search index* + operator + value.
    - **Key insight:** a pre-filter is only as good as the metadata on the docs — you can't filter on a field you never stored. So each PDF must be tagged (`loan_type`, `borrower_type`, `scope`).
    - **Build catch (community):** managed Data Library may not expose custom metadata fields for filtering → likely need a **custom DLO/DMO schema**. Verify early.
    - **Over-filtering failure mode:** universal policies (e.g. general responsible-lending) apply to all loans; tagging must not exclude them.
      - **How (doc-verified):** each chunk indexes only ONE pre-filter value (no arrays) — so don't multi-tag. Two patterns:
        - **A — composite value + IN:** tag `applies_to='secured_business'` / `'all'`; filter `applies_to IN ('secured_business','all')`. Works with a flat filter.
        - **B — separate fields + OR:** `(loan_type='secured' AND borrower_type='business') OR scope='all'`. Needs nested-boolean filter (query-API supports; retriever UI may be flat-only → verify).
      - **Build gotchas:** (1) one pre-filter value per chunk in 1:N; (2) filter VALUES are case-sensitive (field names aren't) → pick a lowercase convention or filters silently miss.
      - **Verify in org:** which operators the agent retriever UI exposes (IN? nested groups?) — decides A vs B.
    - **Two payoffs:** quality (narrower set → higher precision, right-segment grounding) + cost/speed (fewer chunks to vector-compare → faster, cheaper).
11. **Retriever design (doc-verified — Trailhead).**
    - Only policy is RAG → **one individual retriever**. Non-RAG sources (profile/Equifax/financials) reach the memo prompt as **direct inputs**, not via retriever → the "one retriever per template" limit is a non-issue here.
    - **Hybrid index (not vector-only) for policy** — vector search struggles with numbers/domain terms; credit policy is full of exact terms (ratios `1:1`, `$500k`, clause numbers). Hybrid = semantic + keyword, fusion-ranked → catches them. *This is a why-RAG-fails-on-financials detail.*
    - **Ensemble retriever** = the feature for grounding on MULTIPLE sources (individual retriever = 1 index = 1 DMO). Only needed if benchmark reports are added as a 2nd RAG source. NOT the same as hybrid (which is a search *method*, not source-spanning). Ensemble merges + **dynamically** reranks by query similarity — **per-retriever weighting is NOT a supported feature** (verified; an AI answer claimed it, then retracted under push).

